# seq: seq5-symlink-chain
# desc: chained symlink resolution — open-via-symlink to sealed target
#       must follow the seal regardless of which alias is used.
#
# HIGH-8 (mesh Review-1) rewrite: the previous step 3 used op_symlink
# which hardcoded "/nonexistent-mesh-target" AND unlinked the new
# alias before returning. Step 4 then re-probed the ORIGINAL alias,
# making it a duplicate of step 2 — the dynamic-alias seal-follow
# property was NOT empirically witnessed for any alias created after
# the daemon launched. Replaced with op_symlink_persistent which
# points the new alias at $ME18_SEALED_TARGET (deterministic name
# "$WORK/me21/sym-parent/persist-sym", not unlinked) so step 4 can
# probe through it and witness the seal binding the resolved inode,
# not the path.
me21_step 1 a1 open-via-symlink "$ME18_ALIAS"     ALLOW expected-ALLOW-actor-via-original-alias
me21_step 2 b1 open-via-symlink "$ME18_ALIAS"     DENY  expected-DENY-outsider-via-original-alias
me21_step 3 a1 symlink-persistent "$WORK/me21/sym-parent|$ME18_SEALED_TARGET" ALLOW expected-ALLOW-actor-creates-new-alias-pointing-at-sealed-target
me21_step 4 b1 open-via-symlink "$WORK/me21/sym-parent/persist-sym" DENY  expected-DENY-outsider-via-NEW-alias-seal-follows-resolved-inode
